{"name":"Thin Air","hex":"#c6fcff","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"thin air","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An unknown location;  apparent disappearance or nonexistence.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see thin,‎ air,‎ (especially, low-density air as a result of high altitude)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thin_air"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thin Air (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Thin Air is British singer-songwriter Peter Hammill's 32nd solo album, released on his own Fie! Records label in June 2009. It was additionally made available as digital download through Burning Shed Records."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7a/Peter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thin_Air_(album)","wikidata":"Q2628857"},{"title":"Thin Air (Morgan novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Thin Air is a dystopian cyberpunk military science fiction novel by Richard K. Morgan first published in 2018. Set in the same reality as his 2007 novel Black Man, it is set \"well over a century\" later than the earlier novel, with all the action taking place on Mars, whereas Black Man / Thirteen is set on Earth."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e0/Thin_Air_%28Morgan_novel%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thin_Air_(Morgan_novel)","wikidata":"Q65119231"},{"title":"Atmosphere of Earth","blocks":[{"text":"The atmosphere of Earth consists of a layer of mixed gas that is retained by gravity, surrounding the Earth's surface. It contains variable quantities of suspended aerosols and particulates that create weather features such as clouds and hazes. The atmosphere serves as a protective buffer between the Earth's surface and outer space. It shields the surface from most meteoroids and ultraviolet solar radiation, reduces diurnal temperature variation – the temperature extremes between day and night – and keeps it warm through heat retention via the greenhouse effect. The atmosphere redistributes heat and moisture among different regions via air currents, and provides the chemical and climate conditions that allow life to exist and evolve on Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/330px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmosphere_of_Earth","wikidata":"Q3230"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7a/Peter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg/250px-Peter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7a/Peter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7a/Peter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg/250px-Peter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeter_Hammill_Thin_Air.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e0/Thin_Air_%28Morgan_novel%29.jpg/250px-Thin_Air_%28Morgan_novel%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e0/Thin_Air_%28Morgan_novel%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e0/Thin_Air_%28Morgan_novel%29.jpg/250px-Thin_Air_%28Morgan_novel%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThin_Air_(Morgan_novel).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/500px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/1280px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/500px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Earth's atmosphere from space, showing a blue layer in the stratosphere above the clouds of the troposphere. The Moon is visible as a crescent in the background.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATop_of_Atmosphere.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg/500px-ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg/1280px-ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg/500px-ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Afterglow of the troposphere (orange), the stratosphere (blue) and the mesosphere (dark) at which atmospheric entry begins, leaving smoke trails, such as in this case of a spacecraft reentry","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Kittinger-jump.jpg/500px-Kittinger-jump.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Kittinger-jump.jpg/1280px-Kittinger-jump.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe